<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tipu's Tiger</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1795</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tipu's Tiger is a wooden automaton made for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> around 1795, showing a life-sized tiger mauling a European soldier. A small pipe organ inside imitates the growls of the tiger and the cries of its victim. Taken by the British after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srirangapatna">Srirangapatna</a> in 1799, it became a famous trophy of empire and is now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victoria-and-albert-museum">Victoria and Albert Museum</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tipus-tiger.jpg" alt="Tipu's Tiger" width="320" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Victoria and Albert Museum · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tipu%27s_Tiger_front_view_2006AH4173.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
